<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        ══ Shared components ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

        tokens.axaml holds VALUES and the type scale. This file holds the small
        number of COMPOSITES built out of them that are now drawn by more than
        one screen, so there is one definition of each rather than a copy per
        view. Nothing arrives here on speculation: a style moves in the moment a
        second surface needs it.

          Button.act           the app's primary/quiet action pair. Written for
                               the Stores panel (§M4.6) and now also worn by the
                               Epic consent window.
          :is(TextBlock).para  §13 gap 5's provisional reading measure — 12/18 —
                               which that gap says belongs to the system rather
                               than to the one file that first needed it.
          .consent-*           the consent window's own three pieces.

        ══ Why every reference here is {DynamicResource} ══
        This sheet is a StyleInclude inside Application.Styles, and it is loaded
        from the same App.axaml that populates Application.Resources.
        StaticResource wants its key to exist at parse time, which on that
        ordering is not guaranteed. DynamicResource resolves when the style is
        applied to a control, by which point the token dictionary is up. Same
        values, no ordering dependency. (Inside a view's own Styles block —
        StoresView, GameDetailsView — StaticResource is still correct, because a
        view loads long after startup.)

        ══ These classes cross an assembly boundary ══
        Hoard.Auth.WebView references Avalonia and Hoard.Core and NOTHING else:
        no theme, no tokens, no Hoard.App. That is the quarantine that keeps
        WebView2 in one leaf project (§5.1), and it means the consent window
        cannot reference a brush, a font or a size by name at compile time. It
        builds its tree out of CLASS NAMES instead and lets whichever
        application is running supply the appearance — which is the one seam
        that costs the leaf project no references at all. A host that does not
        merge these styles gets Fluent's defaults, which is legible, rather than
        a half-painted window.

        ══ Themes reach the consent window, transparency does not ══
        And it needed no change to make that true. ThemeService swaps a palette
        by writing Color onto the brush objects in Application.Resources, and
        every reference in this sheet is a DynamicResource pointing at exactly
        those objects — so a theme picked in SETTINGS is already on when the
        consent window opens, with the class-name seam untouched and Hoard.App
        still unreferenced.

        Transparency deliberately does not follow it. Only four tokens carry
        alpha (CaptionFill, ChromeSurface, ChromeGround, ChromeRaised) and this
        window names none of them: it is Ground, Well, Surface, Line, Amber and
        the text inks, every one opaque in both states. That is the right
        answer rather than a limitation — a consent window is a decision
        surface with a provider warning on it, and it is also its own top-level
        with no backdrop of its own, so a translucent fill there would sample
        the application behind it and read differently at every position.
    -->

    <!-- ══ Actions ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         The focus ring is a BRUSH SWAP on PART_ContentPresenter, never a
         thickness change (§10.7). Thickening a border on focus reflows the row
         it sits in, and a button that shuffles sideways as you tab onto it is
         worse than no ring at all. It is set on the presenter rather than on the
         Button because Fluent's own state styles write that presenter directly
         and a TemplateBinding loses to them on hover. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.act">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource BodyFont}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13"/>
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}"/>
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="16,8"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="2"/>
        <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- Primary: the app's one primary-action treatment, the same fill the
         Play button and "Same game" wear. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.act.primary">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Volt}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Volt}"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource VoltInk}"/>
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.act.primary:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource VoltHover}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource VoltHover}"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource VoltInk}"/>
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.act.primary:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource VoltPress}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource VoltPress}"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource VoltInk}"/>
    </Style>
    <!-- On a Volt fill the ring cannot be Volt, so it is VoltInk — the button's
         own text colour, which reads as the control being armed rather than as a
         new colour arriving (§10.7). -->
    <Style Selector="Button.act.primary:focus-visible /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource VoltInk}"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- Quiet: sign out, cancel, try again. Not a cancel and not destructive —
         the other half of a choice, reading quieter only because it is the one
         fewer people want. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.act.quiet">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Line}"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Text}"/>
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.act.quiet:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceRaised}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TextDim}"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Text}"/>
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.act.quiet:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceRaised}"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Text}"/>
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.act.quiet:focus-visible /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Volt}"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- ══ Prose ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Paragraph body. 12/18 rather than §3's 13/18 default, capped at 720px by
         whatever lays it out: §13 gap 5 records that pair as the provisional
         answer for a reading measure the system does not yet own, and the point
         of recording it was to stop the next prose surface inventing a second
         one. TextDim keeps a paragraph part of the room.

         `:is(TextBlock)` rather than `TextBlock` for tokens.axaml's reason: an
         Avalonia type selector matches the exact type, so a bare selector
         silently skips SelectableTextBlock and the failure is unstyled text in a
         system font rather than a build error. -->
    <Style Selector=":is(TextBlock).para">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource BodyFont}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12"/>
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="18"/>
        <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextDim}"/>
    </Style>
    <!-- The paragraph that is a finding rather than context. Full ink. -->
    <Style Selector=":is(TextBlock).para.lead">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Text}"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- ══ The consent window ═════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Drawn by Hoard.Auth.WebView, which can name these classes and nothing
         else. Three pieces, and the middle one is the screen.
    -->

    <!-- The window's ground. Set on the root panel as well as the window,
         because Fluent gives Window its own ControlTheme background and a panel
         that paints Ground across the whole client area cannot lose to it. -->
    <Style Selector="Window.consent">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Ground}"/>
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="DockPanel.consent">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Ground}"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- ══ The provider's own warning ═════════════════════════════════════════
         The most important text on this screen, and the reason the screen
         exists: an embedded browser never shows the user a code, so the moment
         they could have reconsidered has to be moved here rather than allowed to
         evaporate because the flow got smoother.

         Amber, because §2 assigns Amber to "attention: warnings" and this is the
         only warning in the application. NOT Flare — Flare marks unread updates
         and the bucket that counts them, and it means that for exactly as long
         as nothing else in the app wears it. NOT Danger either: nothing here is
         destructive, and the user has not done anything wrong by arriving.

         The edge carries the colour and the type carries the weight: the quote
         steps up to Body L on a Well field while the paragraphs around it stay
         at the 12/18 measure, so it reads first at any glance length. A reader
         who cannot resolve the tint still meets a bigger, brighter, inset block
         (§8) — the emphasis is never colour alone. -->
    <Style Selector="Border.consent-quote">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Well}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Amber}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}"/>
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="18,16"/>
    </Style>
    <Style Selector=":is(TextBlock).consent-quote">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource BodyFont}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource BodyLSize}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Medium"/>
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="22"/>
        <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Text}"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- The action strip. Its top edge is the rule that separates the decision
         from the reading, and it is docked rather than stacked so that a notice
         too tall for the screen scrolls UNDER it — a consent window whose
         buttons can be scrolled out of sight is a consent window with no way
         out. -->
    <Style Selector="Border.consent-actions">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Ground}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Line}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0,1,0,0"/>
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="32,16,32,20"/>
    </Style>

</Styles>
